  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances will likely come in tow waves \ntonight, but focused on mostly northwest IA and northeast NE. The \nfirst will be late afternoon and early evening, centered around the \npotential for something close to surface based if enough heating can \noccur. The warm nose is currently working towards Sioux City with \nWayne NE at 85 degrees and a 73 dew point. Latest soundings suggest \nsurface heating into the upper 80s will be needed to break the cap. \nThe best chance for this should be south of an Elk Point to Okoboji \nline. If the cap can break CAPE values around 2500 J/kg are expected \nwith marginal shear and a freezing level around 14000 feet AGL. This \nwould support wind gusts to 65 mph and potentially an isolated \ntornado if a storm can become rooted in the boundary layer. The \nlatest HRRR runs suggest that surface based development may tough to \ncome by with slightly elevated storms developing closer to Jackson \nand Okoboji about 23-0z.\n\nLater this evening and early overnight a wave moves northeast from \nNE and may bring another threat for showers and storms. Currently \nthese should not be severe with marginal shear and instability \nexpected.\n\nWeak north to northeast flow develops at the surface tonight and \ncontinues through Saturday. Model soundings showing a saturated low \nlevel air mass in place tonight into Saturday morning, shallowest \nnear the Missouri River and deepest near highway 14. Given the \nweaker, cool flow suspect that fog will become fairly widespread \nwith some locally dense. Given the cooler northerly flow, this fog \nwill be slow to lift on Saturday morning and will likely become a \nstratus layer through the day Saturday, leading to a cool and cloudy \nday.\n\nA slow moving low pressure will move onto the Plains Sunday into \nMonday bringing the next threat for rain and isolated thunderstorms. \nFor now severe weather looks very unlikely. Heavy rain also looks a \nbit unlikely with CAPE values pretty low. Could see some extended \nperiods of moderate rainfall, but unlikely to make a push towards \nany flash flooding with river flooding also pretty low.\n\nBroad upper level riding will build into the area behind the slow \nmoving low pressure, with temperatures expected to warm but remain a \nbit below normal. The models are very disagreeable on some weak \nupper level energy mid week next week so confidence on rainfall is \nlow.",
